 "revenue_qoq": "FY2027Q1 is anchored on the guidance midpoint of $61.5B (range $60.0B-$63.0B) [Q-1, Q-5]; the GAAP and non-GAAP revenue guidance are identical. The forecast compounds from the last history quarter, FY2026Q4 revenue $54.229B (D-e90aac72b9, FY minus 9M YTD). That quarter had 14 weeks because FY2026 was a 53-week year [Q-10]. FQ1-27 is a normal 13-week quarter, so the reported-base growth of 61.5/54.229-1 = +13.4% is the value that lands on the guided midpoint. Adjusted to a 13-week base ($54.229B x 13/14 = $50.355B), the underlying growth is about +22.1% q/q. That matches the recent run rate: FY2026Q1 +20.6%, Q2 +74.9%, Q3 +73.7% (F-b11cd8f3c2, F-6429def3c7, F-03e050e3a0) and Q4 +30.8% reported, about +21.5% on a 13-week basis. Later quarters slow to +8%, +5% and +3%. This is analyst judgment. Demand is still exceeding industry supply [Q-7, Q-8], management expects an even stronger FY2027 [Q-6], and strategic customer agreements with price bands are being signed [Q-9, Q-12]. Growth is held well below the FY2026 pace because supply limits volume growth [Q-8] and the price bands narrow upside on pricing. Export-restriction and tariff risk [Q-11] is a downside not modeled separately.",
 "gross_margin": "FY2027Q1 uses the GAAP gross margin guidance of approximately 85.95% [Q-2]. The non-GAAP guidance of approximately 86.25% [Q-15] is not used, because the model is GAAP. This is slightly below FY2026Q4's 86.8% (D-eefea057cd / D-e90aac72b9) and above FY2026Q3's 84.6% (F-e021155183 / F-03e050e3a0). Later quarters stay flat at 85.95%. Management states that margins under the strategic customer agreements, even at floor pricing, are expected to be meaningfully above any past cycle peak [Q-9], which argues against modeling a decline within the next four quarters. With supply still constrained [Q-8], there is also no stated basis for further expansion. This is analyst judgment.",
 "opex_pct_revenue": "FY2027Q1 GAAP operating expenses are guided at approximately $2.31B [Q-3]. Non-GAAP opex guidance of approximately $2.06B [Q-16] is not used, because the model is GAAP. On the $61.5B revenue midpoint [Q-1] that is 3.76% of revenue. The model has one opex line (gross profit less opex = operating income), so 3.76% stands for the guided GAAP opex. Later quarters keep 3.76%, which assumes opex dollars grow in line with revenue (roughly $2.5B by FY2027Q3). FY2026Q4 GAAP opex was R&D $1.913B (D-dc1b77fd75) plus SG&A $0.859B (D-6c738561d6) = $2.772B, or 5.1% of a 14-week quarter, plus $524M of other operating expense in the release. Guidance implies these elevated Q4 levels do not carry into FQ1-27 [Q-3]. The pre-Q4 history (FY2026Q3: R&D $1.316B F-aa03ffc666, SG&A $0.407B F-cd4e85786c) supports a run rate near $2.3B rather than $2.8B.",
 "tax_rate": "The filings give no tax-rate guidance for FY2027. The rate used is the FY2026 effective tax rate of 14.8% from the 10-K tax table (periodic.txt: 'Effective tax rate | 14.8 %'). FY2026Q4 implies 14.9% (provision $6,583M on pre-tax income $44,238M in the release). The FY2026 increase from 11.6% in FY2025 was driven by Pillar Two, effective for Micron in Singapore from 2026 [Q-13]. That regime continues into FY2027, so the FY2026 rate is the best available run rate. Cross-check: FY2027Q1 operating income of $61.5B x (85.95% - 3.76%) = $50.5B, taxed at 14.8% and divided by about 1.15B diluted shares, gives EPS of about $37.5. That is inside the guided GAAP EPS range of $36.84-$38.84 ($37.84 \u00b1 $1.00 [Q-4]). Non-GAAP EPS guidance of $38.15 \u00b1 $1.00 [Q-17] is not the comparison basis, because the model is GAAP. The model excludes interest income, which was $575M in FY2026Q4, so this cross-check is slightly conservative.",
 "share_change_qoq": "+0.3% per quarter, from the recent trend in weighted-average diluted shares: FY2026Q1 1,138M (F-eb182a66d0), Q2 1,142M (F-371855689a), Q3 1,145M (F-3d172a9372). The release shows 1,147M for Q4 and 1,131M for FY2025Q4. That is about +0.31% per
1763:In 2026, we executed strategic customer agreements with a number of customers. These agreements included binding commitments for specific contractually enforceable volumes over the multi-year contract terms. Strategic customer agreements often include substantial customer deposits that are returned to the customer if the customer meets the minimum purchase commitments. If the customer does not meet the minimum purchase commitments, we may retain all or a portion of the deposit. In connection with these strategic customer agreements, we received cash deposits of $12.75 billion in 2026. Nearly all of the deposits are scheduled to be repaid between 2029 and 2031. Certain strategic customer agreements also include terms requiring our customers to maintain letters of credit with third-party financial institutions. Our right to access such letters of credit is contingent upon the occurrence of specified events of default or breach by our customers. Letters of credit are not recognized as revenue unless an event of default or breach has occurred. The aggregate amount of letters of credit issued, or contractually committed to be issued, by third-party financial institutions was $7 billion as of September 3, 2026. In the event of customer default or breach, our contractual recovery rights may include proceeds from letters of credit, rights to decrement customer deposits and other contractual remedies.
2344:Our contract liabilities primarily consisted of customer deposits received in advance of us satisfying our performance obligations under our strategic customer agreements. If the customer meets the minimum purchase commitments, we will return the deposit to the customer. If not, we may retain all, or a portion of the deposit which will be recognized as revenue. Deposits are classified as contract liabilities in other current liabilities or noncurrent customer contract liabilities depending on the expected timing of the satisfaction of the underlying performance obligations. Customer deposits do not represent significant financing components because the payments are primarily intended to secure future production capacity and ensure supply availability rather than provide financing to us.
2961:As of September 3, 2026, the transaction price allocated to our remaining performance obligations was approximately $134 billion, of which $13 billion has been recognized as contract liabilities. Contract liabilities primarily consisted of customer deposits associated with strategic customer agreements. Nearly all of the deposits are scheduled to be repaid between 2029 and 2031. Approximately one-fourth of the remaining performance obligations as of September 3, 2026 are expected to be recognized as revenue over the next twelve months. The remaining performance obligations are expected to be fully recognized within the next five years. As of August 28, 2025, our remaining performance obligations were not material.
